  "content": "UAE introduces new rules for Visit Visa Sponsorship\nOnlinekhabar\nMonday, October 6, 2025\n0\nComments\nKathmandu, October 6\nThe United Arab Emirates (UAE) has implemented new rules regarding visit visas, requiring sponsors to meet specific minimum salary criteria to bring family members or friends. The system is categorised into four tiers: first, second, third, and non-relative (friends).\nIn the first tier, which includes parents, spouse, and children, the sponsor must earn a minimum monthly salary of 4,000 dirhams.\nThe second tier includes siblings and grandparents, for whom the sponsor must earn at least 8,000 dirhams monthly. The same requirement applies to the third tier, which covers uncles, aunts, and cousins.\nFor sponsoring friends (non-relatives), the minimum monthly salary required is 15,000 dirhams.\nAccording to the UAE’s Federal Authority for Identity, Citizenship, Customs, and Port Security (ICP), the new rules link sponsorship eligibility with income. Applicants must present proof of relationship when applying for a visa. A valid passport for at least six months and a return ticket are mandatory.\nThe ICP stated that the regulation aims to increase transparency, support economic diversification, and attract talented individuals to the UAE.\nFour types of new visit visas have also been introduced, covering artificial intelligence (AI), entertainment, events, and cruise tourism.\nAdditionally, a humanitarian residency permit valid for one year has been introduced, with possible extensions under special conditions determined by the authority. This decision also allows widowed or divorced foreign women to reside in the UAE without a sponsor.\nAnnouncing the new provisions, ICP Director General Major General Suhail Saeed Al Khaili said the rules were revised after studying current and future trends in residency and foreign affairs at local, regional, and international levels.\nSpeaking to UAE media, Al Khaili expressed confidence that the latest reforms will help improve quality of life, boost trade and transport, support technology sectors, and strengthen the UAE’s competitiveness globally.\nReact to this post\nHot Topics\ngold\nflood\nfake news\nKhumbu\ngen z protest\nChobhar\nConversation\nNew\nOld\nPopular\nAdd Comment\nLogin to comment\nRemenber me\nForgot password?\nLogin\nNot have account yet?\nSignup Now\nOr use Social Media?\nGoogle\nFacebook\nTwitter\nForgot Password?\nSend Password Reset Link\nBack\nRelated News\nUAE tightens visit visa rules for Nepalis\n3 weeks ago\nUAE’s Deputy Prime Minister in Kathmandu; bilateral meeting today\n6 months ago\nNepal suffers defeat against UAE in World Cup football qualifiers\n1 year ago\nIndia, UAE, and Thailand top choices for Nepali tourists\n2 years ago\nPM Dahal leaving for the UAE today to take part in COP28\n2 years ago\nAnalysing the T20I Triangular Series: Can Nepal secure a spot on the global T20 stage again?\n2 years ago\nMost Read\nHighways blocked nationwide as heavy rainfall disrupts travel\nNepalis celebrate Bijaya Dashami, auspicious Tika time set at 11:53 am\nManaslu climbing in autumn starts on encouraging note; Nima Chhiring sets new record\nMonsoon triggers flood and landslide risks in several districts, DHM issues special alert\nClay and culture: The making of Durga idols\nSubscribe to our newsletter\nSubscribe to Onlinekhabar English to get notified of exclusive news stories.\nEditor's Pick\nEditor's Pick\n4 government buildings, including Supreme Court, declared unfit for use\n3 weeks ago\nEditor's Pick\nCharred body found at Koteshwor Bhatbhateni, Gen-Z protest death toll rises to 74\n3 weeks ago\nEditor's Pick\nNo consensus on Hami Nepal’s claim of leading the Gen-Z movement\n3 weeks ago\nEditor's Pick\nDecades-old irrigation projects stall as Madhesh faces drought, food crisis looms\n2 months ago\nEditor's Pick\nNepal’s farming crisis: When plans stay on paper and fields suffer\n2 months ago\nAlso Read\nBlog\nWhen Nepal’s youth dared to dream of a better tomorrow\n2 hours ago\nSociety\nAI and fake accounts drove one third of online buzz during Nepal Gen Z uprising\n3 hours ago\nDisaster\nNighttime vehicle movement banned indefinitely on Hetauda–Kathmandu short route\n1 day ago\nDisaster\nHundreds stranded on Everest’s eastern side after heavy snowfall\n1 day ago\nDisaster\nFloods and landslides obstruct roads at 19 locations in Koshi and Bagmati Provinces\n1 day ago",
